The problem describes a waiter at a restaurant who had a certain number of customers to wait on and then received more customers during the lunch rush. The key information is that 49 customers did not leave a tip. We need to determine how many customers did leave a tip.  We start by calculating the total number of customers.  We simply add the number of initial customers and the number of customers added during the lunch rush. We then calculate the number of customers who left a tip by subtracting the number of customers who *didn't* leave a tip from the total number of customers.

